<%# How the app works, briefly: what a scan does, the two ratings (access and trust) and
    how they combine, and a link to the longer write-up. The tiers, the trust levels and
    the grid are generated from the rule engine's data and ScanRating (HelpController),
    so they cannot drift from what the app does.

    The page is as wide as the scan and statistics pages. On large screens the two short
    sections sit side by side and the access tiers flow into two columns. %>
<% content_for :title, "How it works" %>
<% link_class = "text-brand-700 hover:text-brand-800 dark:text-brand-300 dark:hover:text-brand-200 underline rounded focus:outline-none focus-visible:ring-2 focus-visible:ring-brand-500" %>
<% heading_class = "text-lg font-semibold text-gray-700 dark:text-gray-200 mb-3" %>
<% strong_class = "text-gray-700 dark:text-gray-200" %>
<div class="min-h-screen flex flex-col bg-gray-50 dark:bg-gray-900">
  <div class="max-w-7xl w-full mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8 py-8 flex-grow" id="help-page">
    <%= render "shared/header" %>

    <article class="bg-white dark:bg-gray-800 rounded-lg shadow-lg p-6 sm:p-8 mb-6 text-gray-600 dark:text-gray-300 leading-relaxed">
      <h1 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-700 dark:text-gray-200 mb-3">How CRXaminer works</h1>
      <p class="max-w-3xl">
        CRXaminer shows what a Chrome extension is able to do before you install it.
        It reads the extension's package and its Chrome Web Store listing. It never runs the extension.
      </p>

      <div class="mt-8 grid grid-cols-1 lg:grid-cols-2 gap-x-12 gap-y-8" id="help-columns">
        <section aria-labelledby="help-scan">
          <h2 class="<%= heading_class %>" id="help-scan">What a scan does</h2>
          <ol class="list-decimal ml-5 space-y-2">
            <li><strong class="<%= strong_class %>">Reads the store listing:</strong> name, developer, users, ratings, badges and last update.</li>
            <li>
              <strong class="<%= strong_class %>">Downloads the package</strong> from Google and reads its
              <code class="font-mono text-sm">manifest.json</code>, the file where an extension declares what it needs.
              It also lists the URLs that appear in the extension's files.
            </li>
            <li><strong class="<%= strong_class %>">Applies the rules</strong> to the manifest to rate its access.</li>
            <li><strong class="<%= strong_class %>">Runs an AI check</strong> that compares what the extension asks for with what its listing says it does.</li>
          </ol>
        </section>

        <section aria-labelledby="help-result">
          <h2 class="<%= heading_class %>" id="help-result">Reading the result</h2>
          <ul class="list-disc ml-5 space-y-2">
            <li><strong class="<%= strong_class %>">Access</strong> is what the extension could do if it turned bad. Fixed rules decide it, so the same manifest always gets the same tier.</li>
            <li><strong class="<%= strong_class %>">Trust</strong> is what the listing shows about who publishes it and how it is received.</li>
            <li>The <strong class="<%= strong_class %>">overall level</strong> combines the two, with a one-line headline and advice.</li>
            <li>A low level does not prove an extension is safe. CRXaminer does not review the extension's code.</li>
            <li>Scans are saved, so everyone sees the same result. A scan older than 24 hours can be run again from its page.</li>
          </ul>
        </section>
      </div>

      <section aria-labelledby="access" class="mt-8">
        <h2 class="<%= heading_class %>" id="access">Access: what it could do</h2>
        <p class="max-w-3xl">
          The rules read only the manifest. They rate what the extension is allowed to do, assuming the worst, not what its code actually does.
          Each finding has a tier, and the extension's access is the highest tier found. Nothing is added up.
        </p>

        <%= render "levels" %>

        <p class="max-w-3xl">
          Optional permissions are rated one tier lower, because Chrome asks you before granting them.
          On a scan page, the Manifest tab shows the line behind each finding.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section aria-labelledby="trust" class="mt-8">
        <h2 class="<%= heading_class %>" id="trust">Trust: who stands behind it</h2>
        <p class="max-w-3xl">Trust comes from the store listing. The scan page lists the signals it saw.</p>
        <dl class="mt-4 grid grid-cols-1 lg:grid-cols-2 gap-x-12 gap-y-4" id="trust-levels">
          <% @trust_levels.each do |row| %>
            <div data-trust="<%= row.level.key %>">
              <dt><%= trust_badge(row.level, label: nil, size: :sm) %></dt>
              <dd class="mt-1.5 ml-1 pl-3 border-l-2 border-gray-200 dark:border-gray-700">
                <p class="text-gray-700 dark:text-gray-200 font-medium" data-summary><%= row.level.summary %></p>
                <p class="mt-1" data-evidence><%= row.evidence %></p>
              </dd>
            </div>
          <% end %>
        </dl>
        <p class="mt-4 max-w-3xl">
          Few users, few ratings, a low rating or no update for <%= ScanRating::STALE_AFTER_YEARS %> years are shown as cautions but do not lower trust on their own.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section aria-labelledby="combined" class="mt-8">
        <h2 class="<%= heading_class %>" id="combined">How they combine</h2>
        <p class="max-w-3xl">More access or less trust never lowers the overall level.</p>
        <div class="mt-4 overflow-x-auto">
          <table class="text-sm border-separate border-spacing-1 -ml-1" id="rating-grid">
            <caption class="sr-only">Overall level by access tier (rows) and trust level (columns)</caption>
            <thead>
              <tr>
                <th scope="col" class="text-left align-bottom text-xs font-medium text-gray-500 dark:text-gray-400 pr-2">Access ↓ · Trust →</th>
                <% ScanRating::TRUST_LEVELS.each do |level| %>
                  <th scope="col" class="px-1 pb-1 align-bottom font-normal"><%= trust_badge(level, label: nil, size: :sm) %></th>
                <% end %>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              <% @grid.each do |tier, row| %>
                <tr data-tier="<%= tier.key %>">
                  <th scope="row" class="text-left font-normal pr-2"><%= access_badge(tier, label: nil, size: :sm) %></th>
                  <% row.each do |level| %>
                    <td class="<%= severity_classes(level) %> min-w-20 rounded-md px-3 py-1.5 text-center text-xs font-semibold" data-overall="<%= level %>"><%= level %></td>
                  <% end %>
                </tr>
              <% end %>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
        <p class="mt-4 max-w-3xl" id="help-ai-check">
          The AI check can lower trust, never raise it, and it never changes access.
        </p>
      </section>

      <p class="mt-8 pt-4 border-t border-gray-200 dark:border-gray-700">
        For the full story, read
        <%= link_to "the CRXaminer write-up on Astarte", HelpController::WRITE_UP_URL, target: "_blank", rel: "noopener", class: link_class %>.
      </p>
    </article>

    <%= render "shared/footer" %>
  </div>
</div>
